093","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly deep-equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- {\n-   certainty: 'strongly-inferred',\n-   evidence: [\n-     'jinshu:119:s0254'\n-   ],\n-   id: 'jinshu:119:c2093',\n-   predicate: 'name',\n-   subject: 'jinshu:119:p186',\n-   value: {\n-     en: 'Zhong',\n-     kind: 'alternate',\n-     pinyin: 'Zhong',\n-     zh: '仲'\n-   }\n- }\n"},{"id":"addition:jinshu:119:c2094","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly deep-equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- {\n-   certainty: 'strongly-inferred',\n-   evidence: [\n-     'jinshu:119:s0254'\n-   ],\n-   id: 'jinshu:119:c2094',\n-   predicate: 'name',\n-   subject: 'jinshu:119:p188',\n-   value: {\n-     en: 'Xiang',\n-     kind: 'given',\n-     pinyin: 'Xiang',\n-     zh: '襄'\n-   }\n- }\n"},{"id":"addition:jinshu:119:c2095","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly deep-equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- {\n-   certainty: 'strongly-inferred',\n-   evidence: [\n-     'jinshu:119:s0258'\n-   ],\n-   id: 'jinshu:119:c2095',\n-   predicate: 'event-participation',\n-   subject: 'jinshu:119:p190',\n-   value: {\n-     action: 'Fu Jian’s corpse was exhumed and abused by Yao Chang, the act condemned by the historian.',\n-     kind: 'retrospective-reference',\n-     otherPersonId: 'jinshu:119:p183',\n-     provenance: {\n-       mode: 'historian-judgment'\n-     },\n-     receptionType: 'retrospective',\n-     role: 'deceased-former-lord'\n-   }\n- }\n"},{"id":"english:s0004/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He was filial, affectionate toward his brothers, generous and mild, yet lacked the ability to govern the realm and was often ill. Xing intended to make him heir but hesitated.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0008/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Master of Writing Wang Shang, Gentleman of the Yellow Gates Duan Zhang, and gentleman of the Department of State Affairs Fu Yunwen attended him as lecturers on Confucian learning. Hu Yizhou and Xiahou Zhi joined him in literary gatherings.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0019/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'All the generals urged Hong, “Your Highness’s inspired plans flashed forth like lightning and swept away the vile rebels. You should report the victory in an open dispatch, inflating the tally of severed heads to reassure people far and near.”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0021/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'I failed to strike the right balance in pacifying and defending the people, and so encouraged the rebels. I ought now to take the blame, reproach myself and acknowledge my guilt before the ranks. How dare I boast extravagantly and make my fault still greater?”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0024/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao repeatedly supported Bi, yet Hong trusted Shao wholeheartedly, treated him with the deference due a senior kinsman and bore him no grudge on that account.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0031/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong announced the mourning. In the twelfth year of Yixi he presumptuously ascended the imperial throne, proclaimed a general pardon extending to those condemned to death, changed the era name to Yonghe and lodged in a mourning hut at the council hall.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0047/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He was about to enfeoff sixteen palace officials as viscounts and barons in the five grades of nobility. Yao Zan remonstrated, “The Eastern Palace’s civil and military officers naturally owe it faithful loyalty. They have yet to show conspicuous merit. Why should they receive so many grants of fiefs?”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0053/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Several tens of thousands of Hu households from Bingzhou, Dingyang and Ercheng rebelled against Hong. They entered Pingyang, attacked General Yao Chengdu, who held the title Liyi, at the Xiongnu fort, and proclaimed Cao Hong of the Xiongnu grand shanyu. Everywhere they went they ravaged and plundered.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0054/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'General Who Conquers the East Yao Yi set out from Puban against Cao Hong. He fought at Pingyang, routed Hong, captured him and sent him to Chang’an. He transferred fifteen thousand of their leading households to Yongzhou.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0055/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yang Sheng, Duke of Chouchi, stormed Qishan, captured General Wang Zong, who held the title Jianjie, and pressed on toward Qinzhou.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0057/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Song and Yao Ping pursued Yang Sheng and caught up with him at Bamboo Ridge. Yao Zan hurried to their aid with Yao Qindu, governor of Longxi, Wang Huan, governor of Lüeyang, and the palace guards.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0059/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'When Yao Zan reached Qinzhou, Yang Sheng withdrew to Chouchi.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0065/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Those who understood the signs thought that, because Qinzhou was Yao Hong’s native place, they foretold his impending downfall.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0070/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hu Yan killed the generals Mijie Cheng and Pei Qi, holders of the titles Lijie and Jianwu respectively. Yao Chen, General Who Guards the West, abandoned his garrison and fled east.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0074/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao pressed after Bobo, defeated him at Ma’an Slope and pursued him as far as Chaona. Unable to overtake him, Shao returned.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0078/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong sent Lian Manwei, General Who Supports the State, and Forward General Yao Guang’er to attack Yang Juan at Chencang. Juan fled to San Pass.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0079/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Bobo sent Ti, his elder brother’s son, south to invade Chiyang. Yao Yu, General of Chariots and Cavalry, Forward General Peng Bailang, and General She Xuan, who held the title Jianyi, met his attack and drove him back.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0081/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong’s general Wang Gousheng surrendered Qiqiu to Wang Zhen’e, and Yao Zhang, inspector of Xuzhou, surrendered Xiangcheng to Tan Daoji. The Jin army then entered Yingkou, and many places submitted as it advanced.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0083/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'With a stern expression Dong Zun said, “When kings of old made war on other states, they treated men of worth with courtesy.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0084/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'How can you wage war unjustly and treat a man of distinction in another state with such discourtesy?”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0094/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yi Heng, a gentleman of the Personnel Department, privately told Hong, “Yao Hui, Duke of Qi, rendered Your Majesty loyal service in the crisis over the Duke of Guangping. Since Your Majesty rose like a dragon and succeeded to the throne, no special reward has acknowledged Hui’s devotion.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0107/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Guang took their advice and sent Zhao Xuan south with over a thousand picked troops to defend the Baigu stockade, and General Shi Wuhui, holder of the title Guangwu, east to garrison Gongcheng and bar the Jin army.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0108/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Xuan wept and said to Guang, “Three emperors have shown me great favor. All I can do is die at my post.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0119/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yi took his advice and led troops to Shan Ford. He distributed grain to the non-Chinese and Chinese inhabitants north of the Yellow River, intending to deplete the state reserves, attract the Qiang groups of Herong and win personal credit for the largesse.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0120/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yi’s Left Regular Attendant Zhang Chang and Attendant Gentleman Zuo Ya earnestly remonstrated with him: “Your Highness is the emperor’s younger brother by the same mother. You bear the weight of regional command and share the state’s safety and danger, its joys and its sorrows.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0128/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Just send a swift rider to summon Sun Chang, and send Pacifying General Yao Zan to hold the city of Shan. I will go to Tong Pass to direct the armies. If Chang comes in obedience to the edict, I will have Yi lead the troops now available in Hedong to join us against the invaders from Wu.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0131/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong sent Yao Zan, Champion General Sima Guofan and General She Xuan, holder of the title Jianyi, to encamp at Shan Ford, and Martial Guard commander Yao Lü to encamp at Tong Pass.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0134/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yi then sent the Valiant Cavalry commander Wang Guo with several hundred armored soldiers to attack Chengdu. Chengdu captured and imprisoned Guo and sent Yi this rebuke: “As the emperor’s younger brother by the same mother, you were entrusted with military command. The state now hangs as precariously as a dangling tassel. You ought to serve with reverent care and diligence and uphold the royal house.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0137/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Wang Guo added feet to a snake. He is a criminal against the state and is already in custody. I await the imperial order to put him to death.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0139/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Chengdu sent proclamations to the cities, exhorting them to loyalty and righteousness. He sharpened weapons, fed the horses and levied contributions for the cause.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0148/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'When General Peng Wandu, holder of the title Jianjie, heard that Hui was approaching, he abandoned Yinmi and fled back to Chang’an.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0153/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Jun, governor of Fufeng, Yao Moli, army protector at Anyi, Yao E’du, General Who Establishes Might, and Peng Hao, General Who Displays Might, all surrendered to Hui out of fear.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0154/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hui’s maternal uncle Gou He, then a general with the title Lijie, remained unswervingly loyal. Hong summoned him and asked, “Everyone is weighing whether to leave or stay. How can you remain at ease?”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0155/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Gou He replied, “If Heaven lets this wicked rebel have his way and carry through his revolt, our bond as uncle and nephew needs no rushing to his side to make it closer.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0159/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao led light cavalry ahead to meet the emergency and sent Yao Qia and Sima Guofan with thirty thousand infantry to hurry to Chang’an.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0161/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hearing that Hui was drawing near, Yao Zan left General Yin Ya, holder of the Ningshuo title, as governor of Hongnong to guard Tong Pass and led the armies back to Chang’an.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0162/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong acknowledged his fault to Zan: “I, Yuanzi, have failed to uphold and make known the way of virtue and righteousness or to lead my subordinates. I have let disaster arise within my walls and rebellion break out among my own brothers. I have failed my ancestors and am ashamed to face my uncles.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0165/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Pushing up his sleeves and weeping aloud, Zan said, “Unless the grand general and I destroy this rebel, I will never again show my face before Your Majesty!”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0172/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Tan Daoji and Shen Linzi stormed Xiangyi stockade. Xue Bo, General Who Establishes Might, fled to Hedong.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0173/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Tan Daoji crossed north from Shan to attack Puban and sent General Gou Zhuo to attack Xiongnu Fort. Hong’s General Who Pacifies the East, Yao Chengdu, defeated Gou Zhuo.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0175/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong promoted Shao to grand minister, grand general, grand commander-in-chief and commander of all military affairs at court and in the provinces. He granted him the yellow axe and changed his fief to the dukedom of Lu. Shao retained his offices as palace attendant, metropolitan inspector and director of the imperial clan, his staff of authority and his oversight of the Secretariat. All major matters of court policy went to him for decision.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0187/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Daoji led Wang Jing, Shen Linzi and others in a charge against Shao’s army. Shao’s officers and men scattered in alarm and fell back to Dingcheng.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0193/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yin Ya said, “Surviving the first time was already more than I had hoped for. How could I be unwilling to die now?'\n"},{"id":"english:s0196/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong sent Yao Hedu, an attendant gentleman of the Yellow Gates, to encamp at Yaoliu as a precaution against Shen Tianzi.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0197/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao told his generals, “Daoji and the others have come a long way to their deaths. Their forces are small. They cling to their ramparts to defend themselves only because they want to gain time until reinforcements arrive.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0204/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Shao positioned his armies along separate routes so they could support each other. He sent Hu Yidu, General Who Supports the State, to hold the eastern plain and Martial Guard commander Yao Luan to encamp on the main road, in contact with the Jin army.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0205/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Shen Linzi picked elite troops and had them hold gags in their mouths to keep silent during a night attack. Yao Luan’s force broke and he died in battle. More than nine thousand soldiers were killed.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0207/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong had sent Yao Chen to guard Yaoliu and Yao Hedu to attack Xue Bo in Hedong. When Hedu heard that the Jin army was intercepting Yao Nan, he hurried by forced marches to help. Nan was defeated before Hedu arrived, but Hedu then defeated a subordinate general of Liu Yu’s at the bend of the Yellow River and camped at Puban.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0213/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Shen Linzi led eight thousand men to intercept Yao Qia on the Yellow River. Qia died in battle and his entire force was lost.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0216/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Wei sent Baba Song, minister of education and Duke of Nanping, and Yizhan Juan, Upright General and Duke of Anping, to advance and occupy Henei. Raiding General Wang Luosheng camped in Hedong to support Hong.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0219/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Shen Tianzi defeated Yao Yu. Hong fell back and camped at Bashang, and many commanderies and counties in Guanzhong entered into secret communication with the Jin army.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0220/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Liu Yu reached Tong Pass and sent the generals Zhu Chaoshi and Xu Yizhi to join Xue Bo north of the Yellow River for an attack on Puban.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0222/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Liu Yu sent Wang Zhen’e and Wang Jing west from Qiushe to cross the Wei and press Yao Nan’s army.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0223/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Pu, General Who Guards the East, and Yao Hedu defeated Xu Yizhi and the others at Puban. Xu Yizhi was killed, and Zhu Chaoshi abandoned his men and fled to Tong Pass.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0230/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Qiang, General Who Guards the North, led several thousand men of the commandery and formed ranks with Yao Nan on the Jing River to resist Wang Zhen’e.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0236/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Chen, Yao Lie of the Vanguard, Yao Bao’an of the Left Guard, Cavalier Attendant Wang Bo, Yao Jin, General Who Establishes Martial Might, Yao Hao, General Who Displays Might, Sun Xuan, right assistant director of the Department of State Affairs, and others all died in battle. Hong returned to the palace alone on horseback.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0242/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'His eleven-year-old son Fonian said to Hong, “The men of Jin will have their way. In the end there will surely be no escape. Death by one’s own hand would be better.”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0250/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Historical Appraisal'\n"},{"id":"english:s0251/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'The court historian says: Ever since the imperial court moved to the Yangtze and the prince of the dragon prophecy founded his dynasty there, great raiders have seized their opportunity and refused to be still. Warhorses have galloped to and fro without rest. Dense darkness shrouded the six reaches of the world, and great floods surged to its eight limits. Heaven had not yet tired of disorder, and violent armies were many indeed.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0252/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Yizhong came from the west and gave his allegiance to the Shi house. He spoke frankly to its violent ruler Shi Hu and gave loyal counsel at a court in peril. The plan he left his descendants was to submit to Jin. The righteousness of his dying words deserves praise.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0254/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Chang, called Jingmao, took up the work left by Yao Yizhong and Yao Xiang. Seizing the occasion of the Fu house’s collapse, he called the regional leaders to his side and extended his dominion. Drawing on the force of Murong Chong’s attack, he secured Hangu and the Qin lands with ease.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0255/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He blunted Lei Edi’s attack and brought peace to the northeast.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0257/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He had trees planted in rows to mark his new camp. That, to be sure, commemorated an achievement.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0258/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'But to lay the body of his former lord Fu Jian on thorns and dishonor him in that way—what cruelty!'\n"},{"id":"english:s0260/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Xing, called Zilüe, crushed powerful enemies and carried forward the work his predecessor had begun. With an open mind he sought instruction and made room at his side for worthy men. His affection for his brothers brought harmony to the family, and he governed his subjects by clear rewards and punishments. Men of talent served him with complete devotion, and his fighting men gave their lives for him.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0261/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He took Fen and Jiang and captured Xu and Luo, won the submission of the usurping Yan regime and made the pretended regime in Shu his vassal. He subdued Longyou and pacified Hexi. The people prospered, harvests were plentiful, distant regions were secure and those nearby were united. How could King Zhuang of Chu or Duke Mu of Qin have surpassed him?'\n"},{"id":"english:s0263/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'He entrusted the capital of Liang to Tufa and granted Shuofang to Helian. His insistence on having his way bred disasters. Frontier cities fell in succession, and his rejection of remonstrance invited calamity. Trouble repeatedly broke out within his own house. There was no year of respite from warfare, and the people felt themselves in danger.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0264/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'With such grand designs to pursue, should he not have shown profound favor to his men-at-arms?'\n"},{"id":"english:s0266/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'At a time that demanded action he devoted himself to the pursuit of nonaction. Those clad in fine robes and fed on rich food numbered nearly ten thousand. Analyzing the real and discoursing on emptiness became the general fashion.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0268/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'How much less could a usurping regime, its borders daily encroached upon, bear the labor of construction at Yonggui?'\n"},{"id":"english:s0271/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'With right on its side, the Jin army advanced at an easy pace and took Chang’an.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0275/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Xiang was indeed heroic and resolute; Chang was an outstanding man of treachery.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0276/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Under Xing the dynastic edifice rose high; under Hong it came crashing down.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0039/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Your Highness holds the responsibility of a bulwark of the ruling house. You should consider this deeply.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0009/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'At the time, Master of Writing Wang Min and Right Assistant Director Guo Bo considered the administration of punishments too lenient and discussed imposing stricter rules. Hong said, “When people are thwarted and humiliated, a fierce spirit rises in them.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0056/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong sent Rear General Yao Ping to its relief, and Sheng withdrew.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0072/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao, General Who Campaigns against the Enemy Yin Zhao, General Who Guards the Army Yao Qia and others led fifty thousand infantry and cavalry against Bobo. Yao Hui followed with ten thousand picked cavalry.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0051/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Yao Shao stepped forward and said, “Your Majesty remembers to repay virtue; enfeoffing them is right. The ancients treated such matters with reverence and made appointments at the beginning of the year. You could wait until next spring and then consider it.”'\n"},{"id":"english:s0100/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Hong sent Yan Sheng, colonel of the Yue Cavalry, with three thousand horsemen to the rescue. Martial Guard commander Yao Yinan led ten thousand infantry to help defend Luoyang. Hong also sent Yao Yi, General Who Conquers the East and governor of Bingzhou, south to camp at Shan Ford in support.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0171/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'Mao Dezu attacked Yin Ya, governor of Hongnong, at Li city. Ya’s troops broke, and Dezu sent cavalry in pursuit and captured him. Later Ya killed his Jin guards and fled to hold Tong Pass.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0272/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'The wicked heir lost his plan and surrendered with a cord about his neck, as the Qin ruler had at Zhidao.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0247/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- 'They sent Hong to Jiankang and beheaded him in the marketplace. He was thirty years old and had been on the throne for two years.'\n"},{"id":"english:s0080/literal","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n+ undefined\n- \"Before long Liu Yu, grand commandant of Jin, led a great army against Hong and halted at Pengcheng. He sent Champion General Tan Daoji and Dragon-Soaring General Wang Zhen'e in by the Huai and Fei rivers to attack Qiqiu and Xiangcheng. General Shen Linzi went from the Bian into the Yellow River to attack Cangyuan.\"\n"},{"id":"s0207-Hedu-Nan-callbacks","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly equal:\n\n4 !== 3\n"},{"id":"s0245-wife-individual-span","passed":false,"error":"Expected values to be strictly deep-equal:\n+ actual - expected\n\n  [\n    {\n+     endCodePoint: 4,\n+     exact: '妻子',\n+     occurrence: 0,\n-     endCodePoint: 3,\n-     exact: '妻',\n      startCodePoint: 2,\n+     unitTextHash: 'sha256:62630e83bd0c4743ea87e851de939e9e7f569d0ae365e2303d8c39188f54020f'\n    }\n  ]\n"},{"id":"s0245-source-family-authority","passed":false,"error":"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'includes')"},{"id":"s0254-two-predecessors-no-Fu-Jian","passed":false,"error":"p186 s0254 zh missing 仲"},{"id":"s0258-retrospective-former-lord","passed":false,"error":"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'value')"}]}
